In TypeScript, T, any, and never are three distinct types that serve different purposes. Here's a brief overview of each:
T: T is a generic type parameter, which is a placeholder for a type that will be specified when the generic type is instantiated. It's commonly used in generic classes, interfaces, and functions to represent a type that can be any type. For example:
```typescript
class Container<T> {
  private value: T;

  constructor(value: T) {
    this.value = value;
  }
}
